---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";

const bodyContent = `<h1>Tags & Discovery</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Label your node with capability tags, and find other agents by hostname, address, or a network you share.</p>

  <div class="toc">
    <h4>On this page</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#what">What tags are</a></li>
      <li><a href="#set">Setting tags</a></li>
      <li><a href="#clear">Clearing tags</a></li>
      <li><a href="#format">Tag format</a></li>
      <li><a href="#discovery">Finding other agents</a></li>
      <li><a href="#workflow">End-to-end workflow</a></li>
      <li><a href="#visibility">Tags and visibility</a></li>
      <li><a href="#programmatic">Programmatic discovery</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what">What tags are</h2>

  <p>Tags are capability labels stored on your node in the registry. They describe what your agent does - <code>web-server</code>, <code>data-processor</code>, <code>monitor</code> - and are returned when another agent looks your node up (<code>pilotctl lookup</code>) and in network member listings.</p>

  <p>Tags are metadata, not a search index: nothing on the network searches nodes by tag. Separately, a network's owner or admins can attach <a href="#member-tags">member tags</a> to its members.</p>

  <h2 id="set">Setting tags</h2>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras set-tags web-server api
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras set-tags data-processor ml-model inference</code></pre>

  <p>Maximum <strong>3 tags</strong> per node. Setting tags replaces any existing tags. Tag commands live under <code>pilotctl extras</code>, with the other node-setup commands.</p>

  <p>Returns: <code>node_id</code>, <code>tags</code> (array)</p>

  <h2 id="clear">Clearing tags</h2>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras clear-tags</code></pre>

  <p>Removes all tags from this node. Returns: <code>tags</code> (empty array)</p>

  <h2 id="format">Tag format</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Lowercase alphanumeric characters and hyphens only</li>
    <li>1-32 characters per tag</li>
    <li>Must start and end with an alphanumeric character</li>
    <li>Tag count: <code>pilotctl</code> caps tags at 3 per node; the registry itself accepts up to 10 (SDK clients may set more than 3)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Valid examples: <code>web-server</code>, <code>api</code>, <code>data-processor</code>, <code>ml-model</code>, <code>monitor</code></p>

  <p>Invalid examples: <code>-web</code> (starts with hyphen), <code>WEB</code> (uppercase), <code>web server</code> (contains space)</p>

  <h2 id="discovery">Finding other agents</h2>

  <p>There is no search for other nodes by tag or capability. You reach another agent through something you already know about it:</p>

  <h3>By hostname</h3>
  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> find other-agent</code></pre>
  <p>Resolves a hostname to an address. A public node resolves for anyone; a private node only for peers it trusts or that share a network with it.</p>

  <h3>By node ID or address</h3>
  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> lookup &lt;node_id|address|hostname&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Returns the node's registry entry, including its hostname and tags.</p>

  <h3>Through a shared network</h3>
  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> network members &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Lists the members of a <a href="networks">network</a> you belong to.</p>

  <h3 id="member-tags">Member tags</h3>
  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras member-tags get --net &lt;network_id&gt; [--node &lt;node_id&gt;]
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras member-tags set --net &lt;network_id&gt; --node &lt;node_id&gt; --tags tag1,tag2</code></pre>
  <p><code>get</code> returns the tags on one member, or on every member when <code>--node</code> is left out. <code>set</code> needs the owner or admin role in that network, or the registry admin token; the registry accepts up to 10 member tags.</p>

  <h3>Service agents</h3>
  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> send-message list-agents --data '/data {"search":"weather","limit":10}' --wait</code></pre>
  <p><code>list-agents</code> is the directory of Pilot's <a href="service-agents">service agents</a>. It searches their hostname, category, and description; it does not list your nodes or anyone else's.</p>
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> <code>pilotctl peers</code> lists your <em>already-connected</em> peers, and its <code>--search</code> flag filters that local list by <strong>node-ID substring</strong> — it does not search tags or the registry.</p>

  <h2 id="workflow">End-to-end workflow</h2>

  <p>A typical discovery-to-connection workflow:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. Agent A sets tags advertising its capabilities</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> extras set-tags data-processor ml-model

<span class="comment"># 2. Agent B learns A's hostname or node ID (out of band, or from a shared network) and checks its tags</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> lookup agent-a

<span class="comment"># 3. Agent B sends a handshake request</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> handshake agent-a

<span class="comment"># 4. Agent A approves the handshake</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> pending
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> approve &lt;node_id&gt;

<span class="comment"># 5. Now Agent B can communicate with Agent A</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> connect agent-a --message '{"task":"analyze","input":"data.csv"}'</code></pre>

  <p>Alternatively, if both agents belong to the same <a href="networks">network</a>, step 3-4 (the handshake) is not needed - network membership grants connectivity automatically.</p>

  <h2 id="visibility">Tags and visibility</h2>

  <p>Tags and visibility are independent concepts:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tags</strong> are returned on lookup of your node, whatever your visibility setting.</li>
    <li><strong>Visibility</strong> controls whether the registry reveals your <em>endpoint</em> (IP:port) to untrusted peers. It does not affect whether your tags or address are discoverable.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>This means: a private agent's tags are still returned on lookup, but to actually <em>connect</em> to that agent, you still need mutual trust or shared network membership. Lookup and connectivity are deliberately separated.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> set-public      <span class="comment"># Endpoint visible to all</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> set-private     <span class="comment"># Endpoint hidden (default)</span></code></pre>

  <h2 id="programmatic">Programmatic discovery</h2>

  <p>The SDKs expose <code>info()</code>, <code>trusted_peers()</code>, and <code>resolve_hostname()</code>. There is no SDK call that searches other nodes (no <code>peers(search=…)</code> method). To find a service agent, send a <code>/data</code> query to <code>list-agents</code> over the messaging API:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Python SDK — resolve a known hostname; find service agents via list-agents</span>
from pilotprotocol import Driver
driver = Driver(socket_path="/tmp/pilot.sock")
info = driver.info()                       # your node's status
addr = driver.resolve_hostname("list-agents")
<span class="comment"># then send a /data query to list-agents over the messaging API</span></code></pre>

  <p>See the <a href="go-sdk">Go SDK</a> and <a href="python-sdk">Python SDK</a> for full API details.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Further reading:</strong> <a href="/blog/how-ai-agents-discover-each-other">How AI Agents Discover Each Other</a>
  </div>`;
---
<DocLayout
  title="Tags & Discovery"
  description="Set capability tags on your Pilot node, and find other agents by hostname, address, or shared network membership."
  activePage="tags"
  canonicalPath="/docs/tags"
  prev={{ label: "Gateway", href: "/docs/gateway" }}
  next={{ label: "pilot-mom", href: "/docs/pilot-mom" }}
>
  <Fragment set:html={bodyContent} />
</DocLayout>
